    {
      "id": "47e296f7-1fd0-4db7-a91b-55437f7b21bb",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Overview",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        384,
        160
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 4,
        "width": 500,
        "height": 940,
        "content": "## Google Forms → Calendar → Gmail: Discovery Call Scheduler\n\n### Who's it for\nConsultants, agencies, coaches, recruiters and solo founders who take enquiries through a Google Form and want every lead chased into a booked call  without paying for a scheduling tool. Everything stays inside Google Sheets, Google Calendar and Gmail.\n\n### What it does\nA new form response instantly gets a held slot on your calendar and a personalised email with a one-click confirmation link. The workflow then keeps watching that event and sends escalating reminders until the client confirms  or until it gives up and tells you.\n\n### How it works\n1. **Trigger** — Google Sheets Trigger fires on each new row in the form-responses sheet.\n2. **Config** — one *Workflow Settings* node holds every value you need to edit.\n3. **Find a slot** — reads your calendar and picks the first free working-hours slot that respects your lead time.\n4. **Create the booking** — a Google Calendar event is created with the client as a guest. Its `htmlLink` is the booking link.\n5. **Invite** — Gmail sends a branded HTML email containing that link.\n6. **Follow-up loop** — Wait → re-read the event → has the client RSVP'd?\n   - **Yes** → confirmation email + sheet marked `booked`. Done.\n   - **No** → counter +1 → if under the limit, send the next reminder and loop back to Wait.\n7. **Give up** — after the last reminder the sheet is marked `no response` and you get an internal alert.\n\n### How to set up\nSee the setup sticky note on the right of the canvas.\n\n### Requirements\nGoogle Sheets, Google Calendar and Gmail OAuth2 credentials. A Google Form linked to a response sheet.\n\n### Customising it\nSwap Gmail for Outlook or an SMTP node, change `reminderIntervalHours` to 48 for a gentler cadence, or add a Slack node next to *Alert Host Internally*."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "28dff39d-d199-4f9e-ae37-b2c169aed15f",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Setup",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        928,
        160
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 3,
        "width": 700,
        "height": 460,
        "content": "## Setup — about 10 minutes\n\n**1. Prepare the sheet.** Open the sheet your Google Form writes to and add these columns to the right of your form's own columns, spelled exactly like this:\n\n`Booking Status` · `Calendar Event ID` · `Booking Link` · `Held Slot` · `Reminders Sent` · `Last Contacted` · `Booked At`\n\n**2. Connect credentials** on the Google Sheets, Google Calendar and Gmail nodes (three OAuth2 credentials).\n\n**3. Pick the sheet** in the trigger node's Document and Sheet dropdowns.\n\n**4. Fill in `Workflow Settings`** — your name, company, alert email, calendar ID, spreadsheet ID and sheet name, plus the timing values.\n\n**5. Map your form questions** in `Prepare Client Record`. The defaults assume columns named *Full Name*, *Email Address*, *Company* and *What do you need help with?* — rename the expressions to match your form.\n\n**6. Test** with one dummy form entry, then set `reminderIntervalHours` back up to 24 and activate.\n\n⚠️ The slot finder uses your n8n instance's timezone. Set it under **Settings → Workflow → Timezone** before going live."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "819f0797-5279-4604-b6c8-5df76d9b412a",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Intake",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1664,
        96
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 5,
        "width": 700,
        "height": 240,
        "content": "### 1 · Intake and slot selection\n\nThe trigger polls the response sheet every minute. `Workflow Settings` is the only node you should need to edit. `Prepare Client Record` normalises the form columns into predictable field names, and the Code node walks forward through your calendar in 30-minute steps until it finds a free weekday slot inside your working hours."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "bf8d0382-d8de-42e0-b971-779bf0e79e3e",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Booking",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1664,
        384
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 6,
        "width": 880,
        "height": 240,
        "content": "### 2 · Create the booking and invite the client\n\nThe calendar event *is* the booking. Its `htmlLink` is what the client clicks, so no third-party scheduling tool is involved. `sendUpdates: none` suppresses Google's plain invite email so your branded Gmail message is the only thing they receive.\n\n**Prefer letting clients pick their own time?** Create a Google Calendar *appointment schedule*, paste its public URL into `reschedulePageUrl`, and the invite email will offer it as an alternative."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "13f46a68-fd72-40ac-a547-1fb17ac5bba6",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Loop",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        2640,
        368
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 620,
        "height": 240,
        "content": "### 3 · The polling loop\n\n`Wait` suspends the execution — it costs nothing while idle. On resume the event is re-read and the client's `responseStatus` is checked. Accepted or tentative both count as booked.\n\nIf the event was deleted, the node continues with empty output and the loop treats it as not booked."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "67f11f08-f58a-4901-9494-b0aedf0e1b2c",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Outcomes",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        3536,
        384
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 2,
        "width": 940,
        "height": 140,
        "content": "### 4 · Outcomes — confirmed, reminded, or escalated"
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "25d79383-c4d4-4c81-a5ab-7ad0fef1b4da",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Counter",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        3536,
        1120
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 940,
        "height": 200,
        "content": "### The reminder counter\n\n`Increment Reminder Counter` uses `$runIndex + 1`, which counts how many times that node has run inside this execution — so it doubles as the reminder counter without any external storage. It also picks the subject line and opening line for each reminder, so the tone escalates from a soft nudge to a final warning.\n\nOnce the count passes `maxReminders`, the loop exits, the row is marked `no response` and you get an internal email with the client's details."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    }
  ],
